{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target conflates consistency models with architectural decentralization, then smuggles that conflation into a causal mechanism for correlated-failure resilience. The mechanism asserts: strict consistency 'requires all replicas to agree; a shared cause (like a network partition) violates this, causing a hard systemic failure,' while decentralized services 'can continue operating locally.' But a single large service can run a quorum/Paxos protocol (tolerating minority partitions) or use erasure coding and majority voting, so a partition does NOT necessarily cause systemic failure — it reduces capacity. Conversely, decentralized services on lazy/eventual consistency can suffer correlated hard failure precisely because the problem's key uncertainty (hidden shared dependencies like DNS, identity, shared cloud-provider control planes) can make nominally-independent nodes all go down simultaneously, with no quorum to restore because there is no central coordinator. The target's 'mechanism' never identifies what evidence would distinguish genuinely independent failure modes from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause ones; it instead substitutes a consistency-model dichotomy that is neither necessary (a large service can partition-tolerate) nor sufficient (independent lazy nodes can still share a hidden single point of failure). Thus the causal claim is a category error and the requested discriminating evidence is never produced.","counterexample":[{"type":"single-large-service-with-quorum","setup":"A single large service runs Raft across 5 replicas in 3 data centers with a strict-consistency API. A network partition isolates 2 nodes.","prediction_per_target":"The large service remains available via the 3-node majority; strict consistency is preserved; no systemic hard failure occurs.","actual_outcome":"Quorum continues serving; the target's 'shared cause -> hard systemic failure' step is falsified.","shows":"A strictly-consistent large service can tolerate partitions and correlated node loss, so the consistency model is not the claimed intrinsic coupling."},{"type":"decentralized-lazy-network-with-shared-DNS","setup":"A decentralized network of small services uses eventual consistency. All depend on a single registrar/DNS provider that fails.","prediction_per_target":"Hidden shared cause produces correlated hard failure across independent nodes; with no central coordinator to declare quorum, restoration depends on the external dependency.","actual_outcome":"Correlated hard failure across the decentralized network despite lazy consistency.","shows":"Lazy/eventual consistency does not, by itself, convert correlated failure into soft degradation when shared dependencies exist."}]}]}